import Mathlib
import CombinatorialGroupTheory_Magnus_2004.Chap01.Proposition_1_2_20

-- Declarations for this item will be appended below by the statement pipeline.

universe u

section

variable {F : Type u} [Group F] [IsFreeGroup F]

/-- Proposition 1-2-21: the normalizer of the cyclic subgroup generated by a nontrivial element
of a free group is cyclic. -/
-- Primary domain: cyclic subgroups and their normalizers in free groups.
--
-- Layer triage:
-- `source-facing`: the textbook subgroup `N_F(⟨w⟩)` attached to a nontrivial element `w : F`.
-- `core/canonical`: `Subgroup.zpowers w` for the cyclic subgroup generated by `w`,
-- `Subgroup.normalizer` for its normalizer, and `IsCyclic` for the conclusion.
-- `bridge/view`: the previous item's owner theorem
-- `commute_equivalence_on_nontrivial_elements`, together with mathlib's normalizer-membership and
-- cyclic-subgroup APIs, converts the textbook conjugation argument into the canonical subgroup
-- statement below.
--
-- Domain sampling:
-- 1. `Subgroup.zpowers w` is mathlib's canonical owner for the cyclic subgroup generated by `w`.
-- 2. `Subgroup.normalizer` and `Subgroup.mem_normalizer_iff` are the canonical subgroup-level
--    owner and membership bridge for normalizers.
-- 3. `Subgroup.isCyclic_iff_exists_zpowers_eq_top` is the owner-side cyclicity criterion for a
--    subgroup, so no parallel wrapper for "generated by one element" belongs here.
-- 4. `commute_equivalence_on_nontrivial_elements` from Proposition `1-2-20` is the chapter's
--    canonical bridge controlling commutation among nontrivial elements of a free group.
--
-- Primitive vs. derived:
-- the only primitive public data are the ambient free group `F`, the chosen element `w`, and the
-- nontriviality hypothesis `hw`; the subgroup `Subgroup.normalizer (Subgroup.zpowers w : Set F)`
-- is the canonical derived owner object, so no additional wrapper or chosen generator should
-- appear in the public interface.
--
-- Proof sketch: if `g` normalizes `Subgroup.zpowers w`, then conjugation sends `w` to another
-- element of `Subgroup.zpowers w`. The nontriviality of `w`, together with the previous item's
-- commutation equivalence on nonidentity elements, forces all nontrivial elements of the
-- normalizer into one cyclic commutation class, yielding the cyclicity of the whole normalizer.
theorem normalizer_zpowers_isCyclic (w : F) (hw : w ≠ 1) :
    IsCyclic (Subgroup.normalizer (Subgroup.zpowers w : Set F)) := sorry

end
